  thing those forms depend on — and [38;5;153mapp.js[39m today is a bare [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m at [38;5;153mindex.html:13[39m with no module system,
  so there's no existing answer to follow.

  - [1mA second script tag exposing a namespaced global[0m — [38;5;153msession.js[39m loaded before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m, attaching something like
    [38;5;153mwindow.AppSession[39m. Buys an exact match with the current plain-script setup: no build step, no dependency, and
    [38;5;153mindex.html[39m still opens by double-clicking from disk. Costs a name on [38;5;153mwindow[39m.
  - [1mES modules[0m — [38;5;153mindex.html[39m gets [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, [38;5;153msession.js[39m uses [38;5;153mexport[39m, [38;5;153mapp.js[39m uses [38;5;153mimport[39m. Buys real
    encapsulation and the modern default. The cost is concrete: module scripts are blocked under the [38;5;153mfile://[39m protocol,
    so the page would only work served over HTTP from then on. If you're already serving it, that cost is zero; if you
    open the file directly, this breaks that.
  - [1mA bundler[0m — rejecting this one outright. It adds dependencies and a build step to a project that has neither, for
    one small module. YAGNI.

  [1mI recommend the namespaced global.[0m It matches the existing pattern, keeps the zero-dependency setup, and doesn't risk
  the [38;5;153mfile://[39m regression. And it's the cheap one to reverse — moving one small file from a global to an [38;5;153mexport[39m later is
  a few lines, whereas the storage format we just picked is the part that would actually be expensive to change.
